welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Alex def of political science at Yale  professor Deb's his research interests  include the political economy of  dictatorship development and war his  work has appeared in the journal of the  history of economic thought today we'll  talk with Professor Deb's about his  newest paper living by the sword and  dying by the sword leadership  transitions in and out of dictatorships  welcome professor Deb's thank you it's a  pleasure to be here so give us an  overview of your paper so this pay in  this paper i'm trying to address the  following questions what explains the  length of tenure of dictators and what  explains the process by which they lose  office is it violent or nonviolent do  they end in leaving office and in jail  or are they killed or are they able to  walk free and I think these are  interesting questions because we think  of dictatorships as regimes which are  which have relatively weak institutions  where the leader is not but not very  accountable if we compare them to  dictatorships and the leader can remain  in office unless he's threatened by the  use of violence but there is quite a bit  of variation in cases where the leader  where violence is actually used against  the leader or whether the leader is able  to walk free and so that's what I was  trying to understand and looking at  those questions so what are some of the  key findings in your paper well what's  quite interesting is that what we find  is if we look within dictatorships  military dictators or those who have the  shortest tenure and this is something  that we had a handle on in the  literature but quite surprisingly  they're one they're the ones who have  the worst fate upon leaving office and  that's something that was quite  surprising to me because in our  understanding of dictatorships we would  think that it would be the opposite  because we think of Miller military  dictatorships as dictatorships where the  elites are united and that was the  reason I was given for why they 10  to leave office fast because they want  to avoid a fight they don't like to  fight against each other and they don't  like to fight an uprising and so that  would be an explanation for why regimes  are short and another explanation for  the shortness of regimes is that  military dictators are good at  preserving their fate after they leave  office they have this advantage in  violence and so they don't mind leaving  office because they can secure good  rents after leaving office and so when  we look at the evidence it actually  shows that it's the opposite that  military dictators fair the worst when  they leave office and that was something  that was quite intriguing to me and so I  wanted to get a handle on what explains  that phenomenon okay and how did you  come to write the paper well the project  which I was writing before this one was  co-authored with a colleague of mine at  the University of Rochester hayan  Gorman's and the question we were  looking at was what explains the  Democratic peace and the Democratic  peace is this finding that democracies  are reluctant to fight wars against each  other and there were quite a number of  explanations and quite a lot of  empirical work but there were two pieces  that were missing and trying to build  theories which were how the war outcome  affects the fate of the leader whether  he's able to remain in office whether  he's rewarded for winning or punished  for losing and the extent of that  punishment is a leader sent to jail or  killed or is he able to walk free after  leaving office and so when we looked at  the differences between the dictatorship  and democracy we found some pattern  looking at the post tenure fate of  leader and when we dug a little deeper  that's where we also found differences  within dictatorships between the  military dictators and the non military  dictators where the military dictators  fair the worst and so that really piqued  my attention and made me think about  what could explain this finding okay and  how did you gather the data for the  paper you know I'm sitting here  wondering gee how many dictators are  there throughout the world right so the  data that we were looking at was in the  post-world war two period between 1946  and 1996 and there  about 480 dictators in power at any  point in during that period when were  ousted domestically and so the data you  know a big chunk of the data exercise  was to combine two data sets one was by  my co-authored which looked at the post  tenure fate of leaders are they okay  exiled jailed or killed up to a year  after leaving office and the second data  said was the data set that codified  leaders as dictatorships or democracies  and within the dictatorships which type  is that a military dictator a monarch or  a civilian dictator and so the work that  we did in large part was merging those  two pieces of information but there was  quite a bit more work involved in that  and the codification of regimes there  were a lot of missing observations for  example if a leader comes into office  and leaves within the same year that's  an observation that would be missed in  that codification of regimes and quite a  lot of leaders fall into that category  so we had to basically take the codebook  in and look through the history of cases  and try to see which leaders would match  to what category here okay and you  applied your model to the African  country of lasoo to tell us about it  well so this country gives us a nice  case study to look at the logic of the  model because it has a relatively simple  political history in the sense that the  it was only ruled by three dictators  between independence and democratization  so between 1966 in 1993 so we can look  at how long each leader has remained in  office what has helped them remaining in  a remain in office and and whether the  process of their replacement was violent  or not and so we find the first leader  was a civilian chief Jonathan who was in  office for 20 years and you would think  well if he was in office that long he  may have been particularly powerful  leader but he was actually a very weak  leader and the only the only point that  triggered his ouster was when he  actually wanted to assert assert his  power against the military in the  military worried that he would contract  concentrate power in his hands then  decided to depose him but they deposed  him and let him walk free and in fact  you you hear accounts about leaders who  were upset after chief jonathan speaks  against the new regime and saying you  know why isn't he grateful that we saved  his life we could have killed him he his  regime was repressive and we could have  applied violence against them so you see  that he remain in office despite being  relatively weak and the next leader was  a military dictator laconia who remain  in office only for five years and during  that period this was a period where  different factions of the military would  compete against each other and he  eliminated political opponent opponents  for crimes which they may have been  involved in but may not have been the  only people responsible for and he when  he was ousted it was by other members of  the military who forced him at gunpoint  to announce at the national radio that  he was leaving office members of the  military who were sympathetic to him try  to put him back in office this failed  and the new leaders understanding that  he represented a threat put him under  house arrest and so just these two  examples give you a sense that having  some strength is that can actually be  not a blessing but the opposite for a  leader in that dis leader represent a  threat for the next leader and he might  actually fare worse fate and so that  gives you an inkling to the answer to  the question why military leaders have  this worse fate upon leaving office it's  because they represent threats to the  new leaders and so that's why they would  be either jail or killed so that they're  eliminated as political opponents okay  and so what is the conclusion you reach  in your paper well so the conclusion is  that the one key factor in understanding  the length of tenure of dictators is  understanding that the advantage for  violence is something that's transient  and maybe a military dictator is someone  who's relatively strong overall but  there are moments where he will lose the  advantage for violence and in those  cases these challenges will want to  eliminate him as a political player and  so understanding this fact about  violence  make can give us a handle on why these  leaders which we think of as strong  actually those who have the shortest  tenure because when challengers have an  opportunity they will want to eliminate  them and why once they rousted they were  sent to jail or killed and so yes sir so  I think this gives us an answer to the  initial puzzles about the length of  tenure in the post tenure fate of  dictators okay and in doing the research  for your paper what was one of the most  surprising things you found well so  there's there's quite a bit of work on  how leaders are able to manage the  transition from a dictatorship to a  democracy and what I was expecting would  be to find how military dictators fair a  lot worse than their non military  counterparts when they leave office to a  Democrat but in fact the the difference  on that level is not very high the most  important difference is when dictators  leave office to another dictator and  that's where we find that military  dictators are a lot more likely to be  jailed or killed in their non military  counterparts so that's something I  wasn't expecting from what the scholarly  literature that said about dictatorships  okay well thank you so much for being  here today and sharing some of your  research thank you for more information  about professor Deb's and his work  please visit our website at yale edu  backslash macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you
 

